        "## 1. Business Case: AirBnb\n",
        "\n",
        "Business problem: Airbnb hosts need a data-informed nightly price. Prices\n",
        "that are too high may reduce bookings, while prices that are too low can limit\n",
        "revenue.\n",
        "\n",
        "Goal: Use NYC listing characteristics, location, availability, and reviews\n",
        "to predict a reasonable nightly listing price.\n",
        "\n",
        "Success measure: Lowest test-set MAE, or average dollar prediction error.\n",
        "Report RMSE and R-squared as supporting measures.\n",
        "\n",
        "How Airbnb could use it: Offer hosts a suggested starting price and a\n",
        "plausible range. It is a pricing aid, not a guarantee of bookings or revenue.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# 2. Data Sources and References\n",
        "\n",
        "## Primary Dataset: Inside Airbnb Detailed Listings — New York City\n",
        "\n",
        "File used: `listings (4).csv.gz`  \n",
        "Original source: [Inside Airbnb — New York City data](https://beta.insideairbnb.com/get-the-data/)\n",
        "\n",
        "This is the main dataset used to build our pricing models. Inside Airbnb collects publicly available listing information from Airbnb and publishes downloadable datasets for individual cities.\n",
        "\n",
        "Each row represents one NYC Airbnb listing. Important fields include the listed nightly price, borough, neighborhood, room type, property type, number of guests accommodated, bedrooms, beds, bathrooms, availability, number of reviews, and guest review scores.\n",
        "\n",
        "We use this dataset to predict a reasonable nightly listed price for a property based on its location, size, type, availability, and review-related characteristics. The detailed listings file is used instead of the smaller summary listings file because it contains more potential predictor variables for modeling.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Supporting Dataset: Inside Airbnb Calendar Data — New York City\n",
        "\n",
        "File used: `calendar.csv.gz`  \n",
        "Original source: [Inside Airbnb — New York City data](https://beta.insideairbnb.com/get-the-data/)\n",
        "\n",
        "This dataset contains one row for each listing-date combination. It includes the Airbnb listing ID, date, whether the listing was marked available, and minimum/maximum stay rules.\n",
        "\n",
        "We use this file only for supporting descriptive analysis. Specifically, we calculate the share of calendar days that each listing is marked available and compare that measure with nightly price. Because the supplied version does not include usable daily price information, it is not used for seasonal pricing analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "Availability does not prove that a listing was booked or that demand was high. A host may block dates for personal use, maintenance, or other reasons. Therefore, this dataset provides context about host availability rather than a direct measure of occupancy or demand.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Supporting Dataset: NYC Short-Term Rental Registration Data\n",
        "\n",
        "File used: `ose-str-registration-dataset-2026-11-02.xlsx`  \n",
        "Original source: [NYC Office of Special Enforcement — Registration and Listing Data](https://home4.nyc.gov/site/specialenforcement/registration-law/registration-and-listing-data.page)\n",
        "\n",
        "This official dataset is published by New York City’s Office of Special Enforcement. It contains information on short-term rental registrations and listing identifiers connected to the city’s registration process.\n",
        "\n",
        "We extract Airbnb listing IDs from this source and compare them with IDs in the Inside Airbnb listings dataset. This lets us identify which listings appear in both datasets and compare their median listed prices.\n",
        "\n",
        "A match indicates that the listing appears in the registration dataset. However, a non-match does not automatically mean a listing is illegal or noncompliant because the two datasets can differ by collection date, listing status, platform coverage, or ID format. This section is descriptive and does not claim that registration causes a higher or lower price.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Business Context References\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Skift. (2023, August 28). *New York City Has Approved Only 257 Short-Term Rental Registrations Ahead of September 5 Deadline.*  \n",
        "   https://skift.com/2023/08/28/new-york-city-has-approved-only-257-short-term-rental-registrations-ahead-of-september-5-deadline/amp\n",
        "\n",
        "2. NYC Mayor's Office of Special Enforcement. *Registration Law.*  \n",
        "   https://www.nyc.gov/site/specialenforcement/registration-law/registration.page\n",
        "\n",
        "NYC Airbnb hosts need to set prices that are competitive while reflecting a property’s location, room type, size, and listing characteristics. At the same time, Local Law 18 creates a regulatory environment that affects the short-term rental market in New York City. Our analysis focuses on predicting listed nightly prices, while the registration analysis provides additional regulatory context."
