        "## 31. Conclusions & Recommendations\n",
        "\n",
        "### Best model\n",
        "**KNN Regression is our best model.** It had the lowest test error on all three metrics (MAE \\$93.72, RMSE \\$168.50, R² 0.634). Its typical prediction is off by about \\$94 per night. That is about \\$75 better than guessing the median price (\\$168.73) and about \\$31 better than Linear Regression (\\$124.89). The tuned Decision Tree came second (MAE \\$98.38, R² 0.597). Linear Regression was last (R² 0.472), which suggests price relationships in NYC are not simple straight lines.\n",
        "\n",
        "### What we learned\n",
        "- **Location and space type drive price.** Manhattan listings and entire homes cost the most (Charts 1-2).\n",
        "- **Listing size matters more than reviews.** Guest capacity, beds, and bedrooms correlated with price. Review scores and availability had very weak individual relationships.\n",
        "- **Key drivers in the Decision Tree:** minimum nights, Manhattan location, bathrooms, room type, and availability (Chart 5).\n",
        "- **Price tiers can be predicted.** Our classification extension sorted listings into Budget, Mid-range, and Premium with 74% accuracy.\n",
        "- **Registered listings are priced higher.** Listings found in the NYC registry had a median price of \\$225, versus \\$178.07 for those not found. This is descriptive only. It does not show that registration causes higher prices.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Business recommendations\n",
        "1. **Offer hosts a suggested starting price from the KNN model**, with a range of roughly ±\\$94 around it. It should be presented as a pricing aid, not a guarantee of bookings or revenue.\n",
        "2. **Use the price tiers (Budget, Mid-range, Premium) as a quick market-position check** for new hosts.\n",
        "3. **Show hosts how location, room type, bathrooms, and minimum-night rules affect price**, so they know which choices move their rate.\n",
        "4. **Add a manual review step for high-end listings.** The model underpredicts expensive properties (Chart 4).\n",
        "\n",
        "### Limitations\n",
        "- Prices are **listed** prices, not prices guests actually paid, and the data is a single snapshot in time.\n",
        "- Error grows for expensive listings, and listings above \\$2,000 per night were excluded, so the model does not cover luxury properties.\n",
        "- Availability is not the same as demand, and a registration match does not prove legal status.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Next steps\n",
        "- Add features such as amenities, listing descriptions, and photos to improve high-price predictions.\n",
        "- Add seasonal or event-based prices if a calendar file with daily prices becomes available.\n",
        "- Test additional models, such as Random Forest or Gradient Boosting, and compare against Airbnb's Smart Pricing."
